From b2e93c0e6106c6764153cee0d604a710d4a8946c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Frank Reichenbach <frank.w.reichenbach@web.de>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:15:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 04/11] Treat the values of const, enum, default and examples
 as data

walk() descended into every object in a schema, and _expand_refs()
into every object and array. So both treated a $ref or $id inside the
value of const, enum, default or examples as part of the schema:

- compile() failed with "Unresolvable ref", or with ValueError if the
  $ref string wasn't a valid URI.
- walk() rewrote a $ref inside a const or default value, so equal
  data was rejected and use_default inserted a different value.
- An $id inside such a value replaced the real subschema with that
  $id.

Add DATA_KEYWORDS and SCHEMA_MAPS. walk() now visits what
subschemas() yields: it skips the values of data keywords and
descends into the values of keywords that map names to schemas, so a
property named "default" or "const" is still a schema.
_expand_refs() skips the same values. subschemas() is a generator, so
it adds no stack frame, and the depth limit of walk() still raises
JsonSchemaDefinitionException before Python's recursion limit.

An $id inside such a value, or inside an entry named like one of these
keywords in a custom keyword such as x-defs, is no longer registered.
A $ref to it is now loaded through the handlers, like any other URI;
the CHANGELOG says so.

The tests are in tests/test_ref_data_keywords.py.
